issues.body:
Hi, I would like to move the remaining discussion from [SShakusora/WaystonesSable#7](https://github.com/SShakusora/WaystonesSable/issues/7) over here, since this is now more about a possible Waystones API need than a WaystonesSable-side bug.

First, thanks again for the review in that issue and for adding the new hooks in `21.1.36-SNAPSHOT`. I have migrated WaystonesSable to those APIs in [`f2007755610f71b3559e18cb649d48f18e7722ff`](https://github.com/SShakusora/WaystonesSable/commit/f2007755610f71b3559e18cb649d48f18e7722ff), and removed most of the previous Waystones-targeting mixins.

One small note: I was not able to resolve `21.1.36-SNAPSHOT` from `https://maven.twelveiterations.com/repository/maven-public/` in my local environment, so for now I used a local Maven artifact as a temporary workaround while testing the API migration.

In that commit:

- `WaystoneButtonMixin` was removed.
- `DistanceToPlayerComparatorMixin` was removed.
- the old custom client cache / custom waystone SubLevel sync payloads were removed.
- teleport preparation now uses `WaystoneTeleportEvent.Prepare`.
- per-entity teleport adjustment now uses `WaystoneTeleportEntityEvent.Pre` / `Post`.
- SubLevel waystones are exposed through `CollectDefaultWaystoneGroupsEvent` and `CollectDynamicWaystoneGroupsEvent`.
- Waystone movement during Sable assembly now uses the new `detachWaystone`-based flow instead of patching more of Waystones internals.

That migration worked well overall. However, I found one remaining display-specific case that is not covered by the current APIs.

## Problem

Waystones now uses `Player#distanceToSqr`, and Sable patches that method to project SubLevel coordinates correctly. This works as long as the target SubLevel is currently tracked by the client.

However, Sable only sends full SubLevel tracking data to the client within its configured tracking range. If a Waystone is inside a Sable SubLevel that is visible/logically nearby but outside Sable's client tracking range, the client no longer has the SubLevel pose/plot information needed for Sable's distance patch to project the raw plot coordinate back into world space.

In that case, the Waystone selection GUI still receives the Waystone's raw logical position, but `Player#distanceToSqr` cannot resolve the corresponding SubLevel on the client. The distance display falls back to the raw Sable plot coordinate and can show values like tens of thousands of kilometers.

Increasing Sable's `sub_level_tracking_range` fixes the display, which confirms that the issue is caused by missing client-side display/projection data rather than by Waystones' distance call itself.

## Why this needs a Waystones display API

The actual teleport target should remain the Waystone's logical position. WaystonesSable does not want to mutate the Waystone's real position or alter teleport data just to make the GUI look correct.

What is needed is a separate display concept:

- the real Waystone position remains unchanged;
- WaystonesSable can provide a projected/display position for GUI distance and sorting;
- the display position can be computed server-side, where Sable has complete tracking/storage data;
- the client can use that display position even when it is not tracking the target SubLevel.

This is not something Sable's existing client projection API can solve by itself, because the client may intentionally not know about that SubLevel yet.

## Possible API shape

One possible solution would be display metadata attached to Waystones' existing sync/update packets, for example:

```java
public interface WaystoneDisplayInfoProvider {
    Optional<WaystoneDisplayInfo> getDisplayInfo(ServerPlayer player, Waystone waystone);
}

public record WaystoneDisplayInfo(
        Optional<Vec3> displayPosition,
        Optional<ResourceKey<Level>> displayDimension
) {
}
```

Waystones could then use this display position only for client-side presentation such as:

- distance text in the Waystone selection screen;
- distance sorting;
- any future map/list preview that wants a user-facing position.

The teleport target, validity checks, and stored Waystone position would continue to use the normal `Waystone#getPos()`.

Another possible shape would be an event fired while building the client Waystone list/update packet:

```java
public class CollectWaystoneDisplayInfoEvent extends BalmEvent {
    ServerPlayer player();
    Waystone waystone();

    void setDisplayPosition(Vec3 position);
    void setDisplayDimension(ResourceKey<Level> dimension);
}
```

I do not have a strong preference for the exact API shape. The important part is that it is server-driven and separate from the actual Waystone location.

